    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d283">

                <pb facs="0625" n="613" sameAs="frus:frus1946v09/pg613"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Marshall Mission Files, Lot 54–D270:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">General Marshall to
                        Lieutenant General Albert C.
                        Wedemeyer</hi><anchor n="59" xml:id="d283-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Chungking</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1946-03-09">9 March 1946</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>306. We have a dangerous spot in vicinity of Canton, about 3,000 Communists
                    driven into hiding by Government troops under Chang Fa Kwei<anchor n="60"
                        xml:id="d283-f2"/> who declares them bandits. The proposal is to remove
                    Communists by water to Chefoo. Please query Cooke<anchor n="61" xml:id="d283-f3"
                    /> as to availability of LST or other vessels for such evacuation at an early
                    date. I would suggest that a small port distant from Canton be selected for
                    assembly, medical examination and preparation, etc. General Chou<anchor n="62"
                        xml:id="d283-f4"/> has man in Chungking who knows present hide-outs of
                    Communist personnel.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d283-f1" n="59" type="source" xml:id="d283-f1-content">
                <p>Commanding General, U. S. Forces, China Theater.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d283-f2" n="60" xml:id="d283-f2-content">
                <p>Commanding Chinese troops in Kwangtung.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d283-f3" n="61" xml:id="d283-f3-content">
                <p>Vice Adm. Charles M. Cooke, Jr., commander of the U. S. Seventh Fleet.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d283-f4" n="62" xml:id="d283-f4-content">
                <p>Chou En-lai, Chinese Communist representative on the Committee of Three.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
